  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "Gold competes with cash and bonds as a place to keep wealth. When they pay more than inflation, holding gold costs that real return. When inflation outruns yields, or debts look likely to be inflated away with printed money, gold gains.",
   "data": "Three forces pull different ways. First, the real return on bonds is high: the 10-year real yield is 2.88%, a headwind. Second, inflation is sticky at 3.4% CPI and 3.0% core PCE, so markets price about a 90% chance of a hike by 9 December ( Fed Rate Monitor , 11:15 HKT). Third, jobs are cooling: payrolls rose 29k in September after 133k. The usual template is that tightening into a slowing economy ends in easing, which helps gold. Today differs because inflation has not turned down yet.",
   "unsustainable": "Interest takes about 21% of federal revenue, like a household spending a fifth of its pay on loan interest while its rate keeps rising. These are rough indicators.",
